sliced_code:
### Program Slicing to Isolate Vulnerabilities

**Output:**
```c
	char *line = NULL, unit[10];

	if (sscanf(line + strcspn(line, "@") + 1, "%lf%10s",
		&freq, unit) == 2) {
		if (strcasecmp(unit, "GHz") == 0) {
			freq *= GHZ;
		} else if (strcasecmp(unit, "Mhz") == 0) {
			freq *= MHZ;				
		}
	}
```

**Explanation:**

The vulnerability arises because of the declaration:
```c
char unit[10];
```

The `unit` buffer is only 10 bytes long. However, in the code:
```c
sscanf(line + strcspn(line, "@") + 1, "%lf%10s", &freq, unit);
```
The format specifier `%10s` attempts to read up to 10 characters into the `unit` array, which doesn't leave room for the null-terminator that `sscanf` appends. This can lead to a buffer overflow vulnerability if the input is exactly 10 characters long.

The security patch increases the size of `unit` to 11:
```diff
+	char *line = NULL, unit[11];
```

This ensures there is space for a 10-character string plus the null terminator.

